{Some bidding, huh? Well, what would you have bid in the East seat? West plays 6NT. North leads the \DJ.

Mirror distributions! So you can only make ten tricks in \Hs, \Ds and \Cs. 

That means you will have to make two \S tricks. And since the odds are 5000 to 1 that North has the \SK, leading toward your \SQ is NOT your play.

Win the \D and play 4 rounds of \Cs. North follows to the first, then discards 3 \Ss.

Next play 3 \H winners. North follows twice, then discards a \S.

Now play your 2 \D winners. South and North both follow.

[showcards N:SA,S7,S2 S:SQ,S9,S4]

Keeping your fingers crossed that North must win the next trick you play the \S9. North puts on the \ST, you play low in dummy, and South (thankfully) discards a \H. 

[showcards N:SA,S7 S:SQ,S4]

North has been thrown in and he has nothing left but the \S K J. Whichever one he plays you will take the last two tricks.

North no doubt thought his 3\S bid was a good idea. 

In fact, it just kept you from making a losing play, (leading toward the \SQ), and allowed you to find the end-play to make the slam.}
[Play "N"]
DJ

